\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $4$ and position $2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\frac{4}{2})=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-69718/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be11193b-a241-5812-a1c1-54d233ae9ddd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-69718"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-69663"] :question/problem "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{3}{1}).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $(\\frac{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $3$ and position $1$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\frac{3}{1})=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-69725/selection/7db0a631cab48046c53b4c871078703a318b42bb48461cdd0e554c2956579f09" :answer/id #uuid "99b7ca11-ad01-5c8f-b1ba-11e63c548674" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-69725/selection/f149ee40ceb0119f4abff37e910e97f802f2e08b2a11d3a6aded3c37e40bf7a0" :answer/id #uuid "bcf40022-3d45-5aff-a010-6b955de12c93" :answer/