 in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{5}{2})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{4}{0}) &  & (\\frac{4}{1}) &  & (\\frac{4}{2}) &  & (\\frac{4}{3}) &  & (\\frac{4}{4}) \\\\  &  &  & (\\frac{5}{0}) &  & (\\frac{5}{1}) &  & (\\frac{5}{2}) &  & (\\frac{5}{3}) &  & (\\frac{5}{4}) &  & (\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{5}{2})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2})=(\\frac{4}{1})+(\\frac{4}{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{4}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-69725/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c976b6a-4523-51e1-a243-27aa8937db25"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-69725"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-69669"] :question/problem "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{1})=[]+(\\frac{2}{1})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $(\\frac{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{3}{1})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{3}{1})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{1})=(\\frac{2}{0})+(\\frac{2}{1})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{2}{0}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-113608/selection/822c9f7e46a638c01f074fd2f2efe0ad2bb55d611a3910930a77741dd9180faf" :answer/id #uuid "18408428-cb99-5929-abda-1f295e8b69e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113608/selection/56079b7d9864bca8be4ed3956a02bbba59a88ffb217bad1dd02eff30e5113394" :answer/id #uuid "01ffe23f-d4c4-5529-9e6c-1030767cd140" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113608/selection/87735ab2df82b33e3fe549cf07da1a0df7e3064138a1901262acbdc61d4c0f29" :answer/id #uuid "be9c16a6-f6b7-57f4-8fd8-34c27b5cb6b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113608/selection/711c2b9d81e9ed64077725b05ccf6a44a4d0ad34a2271e4cee09d1e417e23112" :answer/id #uuid "b5f26be9-f5c6-53a0-988e-6df3a1869488" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{6}{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113608/selection/ea8fc46eebbae4d6d956f8a7d5415e4b0dc3ea69a047304eb31dbf828d718922" :answer/id #uuid "5105df09-2e97-5c1c-a3d5-4da5cb0adc61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{6}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113608/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "48239091-3dc1-5d58-86b9-0eac7e555108" :answer-field/key "selecti